A.append(dict(
slug="dental-emergency-guide",
title="Dental Emergencies: What to Do Before You Reach the Dentist",
tag="Emergencies",
desc="First steps for a knocked-out tooth, broken tooth, severe toothache, swelling and lost fillings, plus the warning signs that mean going to an emergency room.",
blurb="Knocked-out tooth, swelling, a cracked molar: first steps, and when to head straight to the ER.",
alt="A tooth character with a bandage beside a glass of milk, a ringing phone and a clock, signalling fast action",
body="""
<p>Dental emergencies rarely happen at a convenient time. A fall on the playground, a bite into an olive pit, or a toothache that suddenly swells your cheek can leave you wondering whether to wait, call a dentist or go to the hospital. This guide explains first steps for the most common situations. It is general information, not a diagnosis: when in doubt, contact a dentist promptly, and for severe symptoms go to an emergency room or call your local emergency number.</p>

<h2>Go to the emergency room or call 911 if...</h2>
<p>Some symptoms can signal a spreading infection or a serious injury that needs hospital care, not just a dental appointment:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Swelling of the face, jaw or neck that is spreading, especially toward the eye or down the neck.</li>
<li>Difficulty breathing, swallowing or opening your mouth.</li>
<li>Fever with facial swelling, or feeling very unwell.</li>
<li>Bleeding from the mouth that does not slow after about 15 minutes of firm pressure.</li>
<li>A possible broken jaw, a jaw that will not close properly, or a head injury, loss of consciousness, confusion or vomiting after a blow.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hospital teams can manage airway problems, severe infection and facial fractures, and they can arrange follow-up with a dentist or oral surgeon.</p>

<h2>Knocked-out adult tooth: minutes matter</h2>
<p>A permanent tooth that has been knocked out has the best chance of being saved if it is put back in its socket quickly, ideally within the first hour and sooner is better. Steps commonly recommended by dental trauma guidelines:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Find the tooth and hold it by the crown</strong> (the white chewing part), not the root.</li>
<li><strong>If it is dirty, rinse it briefly</strong> with milk, saline or cold water for a few seconds. Do not scrub it, dry it or wrap it in tissue.</li>
<li><strong>Try to put it back in the socket</strong>, facing the right way, if the person is alert and it goes in easily. Bite gently on a clean cloth to hold it.</li>
<li><strong>If you cannot replant it</strong>, keep it moist: in a small container of milk, in saline, or in the person's own saliva (for example, in the cheek of an adult who will not swallow it). Plain water is not ideal for longer storage.</li>
<li><strong>See a dentist immediately.</strong> Call ahead so they are ready.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Do not replant a knocked-out baby tooth.</strong> Pushing it back can damage the adult tooth developing underneath. Keep the child comfortable, control bleeding with gentle pressure, and contact your dentist.</p>

<h2>Broken, chipped or cracked teeth</h2>
<p>Rinse your mouth with warm water and save any pieces of tooth in milk or water if you can find them. Apply a cold pack to the outside of the cheek to reduce swelling. A small chip without pain can usually wait for a regular appointment in the next few days, but call the dentist anyway so they can advise. A large break, a broken tooth that hurts, pink or red showing in the centre of the tooth, or a tooth that feels loose or pushed out of position needs prompt care, often the same day.</p>
<p>Cracks are trickier. A tooth that hurts sharply when you bite down and release, especially on something hard, may be cracked. Chew on the other side and see your dentist soon; cracks can spread.</p>

<h2>Severe toothache and swelling</h2>
<p>Rinse with warm water and gently floss around the tooth to remove any trapped food, which is sometimes the whole problem. A cold compress on the cheek may help. Over-the-counter pain relievers can be used as directed on the label if they are safe for you. Do not put aspirin or other tablets directly on the gum, as this can burn the tissue.</p>
<p>Throbbing pain, pain that keeps you awake, a bad taste, or a pimple-like bump on the gum can point to an infection that needs dental treatment. Call your dentist the same day. If swelling spreads or you have a fever, trouble swallowing or trouble breathing, go to an emergency room.</p>

<figure class="table-scroll">
<table>
<caption>Quick triage guide (general information only)</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Where to go</th><th>How soon</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Trouble breathing or swallowing, spreading facial swelling, fever with swelling, uncontrolled bleeding, jaw or head injury</td><td>Emergency room / 911</td><td>Now</td></tr>
<tr><td>Knocked-out adult tooth</td><td>Dentist, or ER if no dentist is reachable</td><td>Immediately, within the hour if possible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loosened or pushed-out tooth, large painful break, localised swelling, severe pain</td><td>Dentist</td><td>Same day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lost filling or crown, small chip, broken braces wire, mild ache</td><td>Dentist</td><td>Call for advice; usually within a few days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>

<h2>Lost filling or crown</h2>
<p>Keep the crown if it comes off. Clean it gently and call your dentist. Pharmacies sell temporary dental cement that can hold a crown in place or cover a hole for a short time; follow the package instructions and never use household glue. Chew on the other side and avoid sticky or very hot or cold foods. These situations are usually not emergencies, but a tooth left open can break or become sensitive, so book soon.</p>

<h2>Soft-tissue injuries</h2>
<p>Cuts to the lips, tongue or cheeks tend to bleed a lot because the mouth has a rich blood supply. Rinse gently, then press a clean gauze or cloth firmly on the area for 15 minutes. A cold compress can help swelling. Deep cuts, cuts through the lip border, or bleeding that will not stop need medical attention.</p>

<h2>Braces troubles</h2>
<p>A poking wire can be pushed gently away from the cheek with a pencil eraser or covered with orthodontic wax. A loose bracket can usually wait until the orthodontist can see you; call for advice. Do not cut wires yourself unless your orthodontist has told you how.</p>

<h2>Be ready before it happens</h2>
<p>Save your dentist's number, and their after-hours instructions, in your phone. Ask at your next checkup what the practice does for emergencies outside office hours. Keep a small kit with gauze, a cold pack, a small lidded container, temporary dental cement and pain relievers suitable for your household. For sports, wear a properly fitted mouthguard. A few minutes of preparation can make a stressful moment a lot calmer.</p>
""",
faq=[
("How long do I have to save a knocked-out tooth?","Time is critical. Replanting a permanent tooth within minutes gives the best chance; aim to see a dentist within the first hour and keep the tooth moist in milk or saliva until then."),
("Should I go to the ER or a dentist for a toothache?","A toothache usually needs a dentist. Go to the ER if you have spreading swelling, fever, trouble breathing or swallowing, uncontrolled bleeding or a jaw or head injury."),
("Can I put a knocked-out baby tooth back?","No. Replanting a baby tooth can damage the adult tooth underneath. Control bleeding and contact your dentist."),
("Is a lost filling an emergency?","Usually not, but call your dentist for advice and book soon, as an open tooth can break or become sensitive."),
],
related=["root-canal-treatment-explained","kids-teeth-care-by-age","how-to-choose-a-dentist"],
))

A.append(dict(
slug="understanding-dental-costs",
title="Paying for Dental Care: Cost Factors, Insurance Terms and Options",
tag="Paying for care",
desc="What drives the cost of dental treatment, how dental insurance terms like deductibles and annual maximums work, and lower-cost options such as dental schools and health centers.",
blurb="Deductibles, annual maximums and treatment estimates decoded, plus lower-cost places to look.",
alt="A piggy bank shaped like a tooth next to a calculator and a long itemised receipt with blank lines",
body="""
<p>Dental bills can be confusing. Two people can have "a filling" and pay very different amounts, and insurance statements are full of terms that sound similar but mean different things. This guide does not quote prices, because fees vary widely by location, practice, material and the specifics of your mouth. Instead, it explains what drives costs, how dental insurance generally works, and the questions that help you avoid surprises.</p>

<h2>What makes dental treatment cost more or less</h2>
<p>Every dental procedure has a code, and each practice sets its own fee for each code. The final amount you pay depends on several factors:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Location.</strong> Practice overheads such as rent and wages differ between cities, suburbs and rural areas.</li>
<li><strong>The exact procedure.</strong> A filling on one surface of a tooth is different from one covering three surfaces. A root canal on a molar with several canals usually involves more work than one on a front tooth.</li>
<li><strong>Materials and lab work.</strong> Crowns, bridges, dentures and implant parts often involve a dental laboratory and materials with different costs.</li>
<li><strong>Who provides care.</strong> Specialists such as endodontists, oral surgeons and periodontists typically have separate fee schedules from general dentists.</li>
<li><strong>Extra steps.</strong> X-rays, a core buildup under a crown, sedation, or a temporary restoration can each be separate line items.</li>
<li><strong>Your coverage.</strong> In-network dentists have agreed fees with your insurer; out-of-network dentists may charge more, and you may owe the difference.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because of all these variables, the most reliable number is a written estimate from the practice for your specific treatment plan.</p>

<h2>Dental insurance vocabulary, decoded</h2>
<figure class="table-scroll">
<table>
<caption>Common dental plan terms</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Premium</td><td>What you pay, often monthly, to keep the plan active, whether or not you use it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deductible</td><td>The amount you pay yourself each plan year before the plan starts sharing costs for certain services. Preventive care is often exempt.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coinsurance</td><td>Your percentage share of a covered service after the deductible.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copay</td><td>A fixed amount you pay for a service in some plan types.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annual maximum</td><td>The most the plan will pay in a plan year. After that, you pay the full remaining cost.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waiting period</td><td>A period after enrolling before certain services, often major ones, are covered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frequency limit</td><td>How often a service is covered, such as a set number of cleanings per year.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing tooth clause</td><td>A rule in some plans that excludes replacing teeth lost before coverage began.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-treatment estimate</td><td>A request the dentist sends the insurer before treatment to learn what will likely be covered.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>

<h2>How dental plans usually differ from medical plans</h2>
<p>Dental insurance often behaves less like catastrophic coverage and more like a budget for routine care. Many plans group services into tiers, usually preventive (exams, cleanings, X-rays), basic (fillings, simple extractions) and major (crowns, bridges, dentures), and cover them at different percentages, with preventive care usually covered most generously. The annual maximum is the key number to know: once the plan has paid out that amount for the year, the rest is up to you. If you are planning substantial treatment, it can sometimes make sense to ask whether work can be safely split across two plan years, but never delay urgent treatment for this reason.</p>
<p>Plan designs vary a lot, and the details are in your plan's summary of benefits. Your employer's benefits team or the insurer's member services line can answer specific coverage questions.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before treatment</h2>
<figure class="box">
<h3>Before you say yes to a treatment plan</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Can I have a written treatment plan with procedure codes and an itemised estimate?</li>
<li>Which items are urgent, and which can safely be scheduled later?</li>
<li>Will you send a pre-treatment estimate to my insurer?</li>
<li>Are you in network with my plan, and is the specialist you refer me to?</li>
<li>Are there alternative treatments, and how do their costs and lifespans compare?</li>
<li>Do you offer payment plans or an in-house membership plan?</li>
<li>If financing is suggested, what are the interest rate, promotional period and terms after it ends?</li>
</ul>
</figure>
<p>Be especially careful with medical credit cards offered at the front desk. Some carry deferred interest: if the full balance is not paid by the end of a promotional period, interest may be charged back to the original purchase date. Read the terms before signing, and do not feel pressured to decide on the spot.</p>

<h2>Options if you have no dental insurance</h2>
<p><strong>In-house membership plans.</strong> Many practices offer an annual membership that includes preventive visits and a discount on other treatment. These are not insurance, but they can make routine care predictable.</p>
<p><strong>Dental discount plans.</strong> Sold by companies that negotiate reduced fees with participating dentists. You pay a membership fee and receive discounts at those dentists. Check which local practices participate before buying, and be aware that these are not insurance.</p>
<p><strong>Dental schools.</strong> Students treat patients under supervision by licensed faculty, often at reduced fees. Appointments usually take longer, and there may be screening or waiting lists. Dental hygiene schools may offer low-cost cleanings.</p>
<p><strong>Community health centers.</strong> Federally funded health centers in the US often provide dental care with fees on a sliding scale based on income. You can search for a center near you through the federal Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA) locator.</p>
<p><strong>Public programs.</strong> In the US, Medicaid and the Children's Health Insurance Program (CHIP) include dental benefits for children. Adult dental coverage under Medicaid varies by state. Original Medicare generally does not cover routine dental care, though some Medicare Advantage plans include dental benefits.</p>

<h2>Preventive care is the best cost control</h2>
<p>It is not a slogan: small problems are simpler and less costly to fix than large ones. A cavity found early may need a small filling; left alone it can grow into something needing a root canal and crown. Keeping up with checkups, brushing with fluoride toothpaste, cleaning between teeth and limiting sugary snacks and drinks are the most reliable ways to keep dental spending down over time.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Why can't this site tell me what a crown or filling costs?","Fees vary by location, practice, materials and your specific tooth, so any single number would be misleading. Ask the practice for a written, itemised estimate."),
("What is an annual maximum?","It is the most a dental plan will pay toward your care in a plan year. Costs beyond it are your responsibility."),
("Are dental discount plans the same as insurance?","No. They give discounted fees at participating dentists in exchange for a membership fee, but they are not insurance."),
("Where can I find lower-cost dental care?","Options include dental school clinics, community health centers with sliding-scale fees, and public programs such as Medicaid and CHIP where eligible."),
],
related=["how-to-choose-a-dentist","dental-crowns-explained","teeth-cleaning-explained"],
))

A.append(dict(
slug="managing-dental-anxiety",
title="Dental Anxiety: Practical Ways to Make Appointments Easier",
tag="Visits",
desc="Why dental anxiety is so common, how to talk to your dental team about it, practical coping techniques, and what sedation options exist.",
blurb="Hand signals, headphones, breathing and honest conversations that make the chair less daunting.",
alt="A relaxed tooth character wearing headphones in a dental chair, with calm waves and a raised-hand stop signal",
body="""
<p>If the thought of a dental appointment makes your stomach knot, you are far from alone. Dental anxiety ranges from mild unease to a fear strong enough that people avoid care for years. The trouble is that avoidance can let small problems grow, which can lead to more complex treatment, which feeds the fear. The good news is that there are many practical ways to break the cycle, and dental teams are used to helping nervous patients.</p>
<p>This article offers general coping strategies. If anxiety is severe or affects many areas of your life, a doctor or mental health professional can offer support, including therapies designed for specific fears.</p>

<h2>Where dental anxiety comes from</h2>
<p>Common roots include a painful or frightening experience in the past, particularly in childhood; fear of needles or of gagging; embarrassment about the state of your teeth; feeling out of control while lying back with someone working in your mouth; and sensory triggers such as the sound of the drill or the smell of the office. Knowing your own triggers is useful, because each one has different fixes.</p>

<h2>Tell the team, early and specifically</h2>
<p>The single most helpful step is also the simplest: tell the practice you are anxious, preferably when you book. A good team will build in extra time, explain what they are doing and check in with you more often. Being specific helps: "I'm fine with cleanings but panic about injections" or "I gag easily" gives them something concrete to plan around.</p>
<p>If you are embarrassed about not having been in a long time, it may help to know that dental teams see this frequently. Their job is to help from wherever you are starting, not to judge how you got there.</p>

<figure class="box">
<h3>Things you can ask for</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>A hand signal, like raising your left hand, that means "please stop," and a promise they will pause.</li>
<li>A "tell-show-do" approach: explain each step, show the instrument, then start.</li>
<li>Numbing gel before any injection, and a slow, gentle injection.</li>
<li>Short breaks during longer procedures to close your mouth and stretch your jaw.</li>
<li>Permission to wear headphones or earbuds with your own music or podcast.</li>
<li>Sunglasses to reduce the glare of the overhead light.</li>
<li>A first appointment that is just a conversation, with no treatment.</li>
<li>The first appointment of the day, so you are not sitting in the waiting room worrying.</li>
</ul>
</figure>

<h2>Coping techniques you can practise</h2>
<p><strong>Slow breathing.</strong> Anxiety speeds up breathing, which can make you feel lightheaded and more panicky. Try breathing in through your nose for a count of four and out slowly for a count of six. Practise at home so it feels automatic in the chair.</p>
<p><strong>Muscle release.</strong> Notice where you hold tension, often the shoulders, hands and feet. Deliberately tense a muscle group for a few seconds, then let it go. Wiggling your toes is an easy, invisible way to stay grounded.</p>
<p><strong>Distraction.</strong> Music, an audiobook or a podcast gives your brain something else to follow. Some people count ceiling tiles or mentally plan a route they know well.</p>
<p><strong>Gag reflex tips.</strong> Breathe through your nose, keep your feet moving, and ask the team to work from front to back or to keep the chair a little more upright. Some people find humming quietly helps.</p>
<p><strong>Bring support.</strong> Ask whether a friend or family member can come with you, at least into the waiting area.</p>

<h2>Start small and build up</h2>
<p>If it has been a long time, you do not have to tackle everything at once. Many people find it easier to start with a consultation and an exam, then a cleaning, then any needed treatment, each visit building confidence. Ask your dentist to prioritise what is urgent and schedule the rest in manageable steps. Treating the most painful problem first can also make the next visits feel less loaded.</p>

<h2>Sedation options</h2>
<p>For some people, techniques alone are not enough. Dentists may offer different levels of sedation, depending on their training and local regulations:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> ("laughing gas"), breathed through a small nose mask, produces a relaxed feeling and wears off quickly after the mask is removed.</li>
<li><strong>Oral sedation</strong>, a prescribed tablet taken before the appointment, makes you drowsy but usually still able to respond. You will need someone to drive you home.</li>
<li><strong>IV sedation</strong>, given through a vein by a dentist with appropriate training and permits, produces deeper relaxation.</li>
<li><strong>General anaesthesia</strong>, where you are fully asleep, is used in specific situations and requires an appropriately trained team and setting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Sedation has benefits and risks, depends on your medical history, and may carry extra costs that insurance does not always cover. Discuss the options and your health conditions and medicines in detail with your dentist before deciding.</p>

<h2>After the appointment</h2>
<p>Notice what went better than you feared, and tell the team what helped so they can repeat it next time. Book your next visit before you leave, while the experience is fresh; waiting until later gives anxiety room to grow. Over time, regular visits usually mean shorter, simpler appointments, which is one of the most effective ways to make dental care feel less daunting.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Is dental anxiety common?","Yes. Many people feel some level of anxiety about dental visits, and dental teams regularly adapt care for nervous patients."),
("Can I ask the dentist to stop during treatment?","Yes. Agree on a hand signal beforehand that means pause, and the team can stop when you raise it."),
("Will I be able to drive after sedation?","After nitrous oxide many people can drive once it wears off, but oral or IV sedation usually requires someone else to drive you. Follow your dentist's instructions."),
("What if I have not seen a dentist in years?","Start with a consultation and exam, explain your worries, and ask the dentist to prioritise treatment into manageable steps."),
],
related=["what-happens-at-a-dental-checkup","how-to-choose-a-dentist","kids-first-dental-visit"],
))

A.append(dict(
slug="brushing-and-flossing-guide",
title="Brushing and Flossing Properly: A Simple Daily Routine That Works",
tag="Everyday care",
desc="How to brush and clean between teeth effectively: technique, timing, fluoride, choosing a toothbrush, floss versus interdental brushes and water flossers.",
blurb="Angles, timing and tools for a two-minute routine that actually reaches the plaque.",
alt="A toothbrush angled at the gumline of a tooth, a floss strand curved in a C-shape, and a two-minute sand timer",
body="""
<p>Brushing and cleaning between your teeth are the foundation of dental health. They remove plaque, the sticky film of bacteria behind both cavities and gum disease. Most people brush every day, yet many still miss the same spots, press too hard or skip between-teeth cleaning entirely. A few adjustments can make your daily routine far more effective without taking much more time.</p>

<h2>The basics: how often and how long</h2>
<p>Dental organisations recommend brushing twice a day for about two minutes each time, using a fluoride toothpaste, and cleaning between your teeth once a day. Two minutes feels longer than you think; many people brush for well under a minute. A phone timer, a song or an electric toothbrush with a built-in timer helps.</p>
<p>Brushing before bed is especially important, because saliva flow drops during sleep and bacteria have hours to work undisturbed.</p>

<h2>Brushing technique, step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Angle the bristles</strong> at about 45 degrees toward the gumline. Plaque collects where the tooth meets the gum.</li>
<li><strong>Use gentle, short strokes</strong> or small circles, moving the brush a tooth or two at a time. With an electric brush, guide the head slowly from tooth to tooth and let it do the work.</li>
<li><strong>Cover every surface</strong>: the outer (cheek) side, the inner (tongue) side and the chewing surfaces. Tilt the brush vertically to clean the backs of the front teeth.</li>
<li><strong>Follow a route.</strong> Start in the same place every time, for example upper right outer surfaces, and work around the mouth so you do not skip areas.</li>
<li><strong>Brush your tongue</strong> gently, or use a tongue scraper, to reduce bacteria and freshen breath.</li>
<li><strong>Spit, do not rinse.</strong> Spitting out excess toothpaste but not rinsing with water leaves a little fluoride on your teeth to keep working.</li>
</ol>
<p>Scrubbing hard does not clean better. Excessive force, especially with a medium or hard brush, can wear enamel near the gumline and contribute to gum recession. Most people do best with a soft-bristled brush. If your bristles flatten and splay within weeks, you are probably pressing too hard.</p>

<h2>Choosing your tools</h2>
<p><strong>Manual or electric?</strong> Both can clean well with good technique. Powered brushes, particularly oscillating-rotating types, have been shown in research reviews to reduce plaque and gingivitis a bit more than manual brushing on average, and they can make it easier for people with limited dexterity, braces or arthritis. The best brush is one you will use properly twice a day.</p>
<p><strong>Toothpaste.</strong> Choose one with fluoride. Look for the ADA Seal of Acceptance if you are in the US, which indicates the product's claims have been evaluated. Specialised pastes exist for sensitivity, tartar control or gum health; ask your dentist whether one suits you.</p>
<p><strong>Replacement.</strong> Change your toothbrush or brush head every three to four months, or sooner if the bristles are frayed, and after an illness if you prefer.</p>

<figure class="table-scroll">
<table>
<caption>Cleaning between teeth: which tool suits whom?</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Tips</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>String floss</td><td>Tight contacts between teeth</td><td>Curve into a C-shape around each tooth and slide gently under the gumline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Floss picks</td><td>People who find wrapping floss awkward</td><td>Use a clean section for each space if possible; same C-shape motion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interdental brushes</td><td>Larger gaps, gum recession, bridges, braces</td><td>Pick a size that fits snugly without forcing; your hygienist can size them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water flosser</td><td>Braces, implants, bridges, limited dexterity</td><td>Aim at the gumline and pause between each tooth; can complement other tools</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>

<h2>How to floss properly</h2>
<p>Break off roughly 45 centimetres (about 18 inches) of floss and wind most of it around one middle finger, and a little around the other. Hold a short section tightly between thumbs and index fingers. Slide it gently between two teeth using a back-and-forth motion; do not snap it into the gum. Curve the floss into a C-shape around one tooth and move it up and down, going slightly below the gumline. Then curve it around the neighbouring tooth and repeat. Unwind a fresh section as you move along, and do not forget the back side of the last tooth in each row.</p>
<p>Gums that are not used to flossing may bleed for the first week or two. That usually settles as inflammation improves. If bleeding continues beyond a couple of weeks, mention it to your dentist or hygienist.</p>
<p>Timing does not matter much. Some people prefer cleaning between teeth before brushing so fluoride toothpaste reaches the freshly cleared spaces; the key is doing it once a day, every day.</p>

<h2>Habits that support your routine</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Keep sugary and acidic snacks and drinks to mealtimes rather than sipping or grazing all day.</li>
<li>After acidic food or drink, wait a little before brushing and rinse with water in the meantime.</li>
<li>Drink water through the day, ideally fluoridated tap water where available.</li>
<li>Chewing sugar-free gum after meals can boost saliva flow.</li>
<li>Do not use your teeth as tools to open packets or bottles.</li>
<li>Keep regular dental checkups and ask your hygienist to point out spots you miss.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Special situations</h2>
<p><strong>Braces.</strong> Food traps easily around brackets. Use a brush angled above and below the brackets, and consider interdental brushes, floss threaders or a water flosser. Your orthodontist can show you specific techniques.</p>
<p><strong>Sensitive teeth.</strong> Use a soft brush and gentle pressure, and try a toothpaste made for sensitivity, used consistently for a few weeks. Sudden or sharp sensitivity in one tooth can signal a cavity or crack, so get it checked.</p>
<p><strong>Dry mouth.</strong> Many medications reduce saliva, which raises cavity risk. Sip water, avoid sugary lozenges and ask your dentist about higher-fluoride products.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Should I brush before or after breakfast?","Either can work. If you brush after acidic foods or drinks, it may help to rinse with water and wait a little first. The most important thing is brushing twice daily."),
("Is an electric toothbrush better than a manual one?","Both can be effective. Some studies show powered brushes remove slightly more plaque on average, and they can be easier for people with braces or limited dexterity."),
("Why do my gums bleed when I floss?","Bleeding often signals gum inflammation. It usually improves within one or two weeks of daily cleaning; see your dentist if it does not."),
("Should I rinse after brushing?","Spitting out excess toothpaste without rinsing leaves more fluoride on your teeth, which can help protect them."),
],
related=["teeth-cleaning-explained","kids-teeth-care-by-age","dental-fillings-explained"],
))
